markdown
# Debug a failure

A test failed. This is the path from that line of output to knowing why,
in order of increasing cost.

## Reproduce it first

Every failure prints its seed and a reproduce command:

```
FAIL  dma/burst_wrap  seed=77120493  6.8s
  UVM_ERROR @ 12480ns: sb miscompare, expected 0x3A got 0x1C
  log:        build/dma/fast/runs/burst_wrap_77120493/sim.log
  reproduce:  ingot sim dma -t burst_wrap -s 77120493
  debug:      ingot sim dma -t burst_wrap -s 77120493 -p debug --waves
```

Run the reproduce command before anything else:

```bash
ingot sim dma -t burst_wrap -s 77120493
```

If it fails the same way, you have a deterministic failure and everything
below applies. If it passes, see
[When the seed doesn't reproduce](#when-the-seed-doesnt-reproduce).

## Read the log

The failure line is the first error, not necessarily the root cause. Open
the log and look at what preceded it:

```bash
less build/dma/fast/runs/burst_wrap_77120493/sim.log
```

Search backward from the error for `UVM_WARNING`, unexpected state
transitions, or the last transaction the scoreboard accepted. Most
miscompares have a visible cause a few hundred nanoseconds earlier.

!!! tip "Grep the log before opening it"

    `grep -n "UVM_\(WARNING\|ERROR\)" sim.log` gives you every report with
    line numbers. Reading those in order is usually faster than paging.

## Get waveforms

If the log doesn't settle it, rerun with the debug profile:

```bash
ingot sim dma -t burst_wrap -s 77120493 -p debug --waves
```

```
  vcs elaborate    58.0s  ok
FAIL  dma/burst_wrap  seed=77120493  9.1s
  waves:      build/dma/debug/runs/burst_wrap_77120493/dump.fsdb
```

Only elaboration ran — analysis was reused. The debug binary has
`-debug_access+all` and `-kdb`, so the FSDB carries everything Verdi
needs.

## Open in Verdi

```bash
verdi -ssf build/dma/debug/runs/burst_wrap_77120493/dump.fsdb &
```

Jump to the error time from the log — `12480ns` in the example — and
trace backward from the miscompared signal.

=== "Narrow the dump first"

    A full-hierarchy dump on a long test can be gigabytes. Scope it:

```bash
    ingot sim dma -t burst_wrap -s 77120493 -p debug --waves \
        +wave_scope=dma_tb.dut.u_engine +wave_start=12000ns
```

    Those plusargs go to the testbench's wave manager, not to VCS.

=== "Log-only debug"

    If Verdi is unavailable, `-p debug` still helps: the binary has full
    debug access, so `$display` hierarchy references that were optimized
    away in `fast` will work.

## Fix, then regress

After the fix, confirm the seed passes, then run the block's testlist to
make sure nothing else regressed:

```bash
ingot sim dma -t burst_wrap -s 77120493
ingot regress dma -p fast
```

The regression uses the `fast` profile. Don't leave the debug artifact
as the default — it's slower to simulate.

## When the seed doesn't reproduce

A seed that fails once and passes on rerun means the testbench has a
source of nondeterminism. Common causes, in order of likelihood:

1. **Wall-clock dependence** — `$time`-independent randomness, `$urandom`
   without a seed, file reads in an unstable order.
2. **Race conditions** — stimulus driven at the clock edge with blocking
   assignments. See [SystemVerilog conventions](../reference/systemverilog.md).
3. **Uninitialized state** — a register the testbench reads before reset
   deasserts.

None of these are fixable by rerunning. Find the nondeterminism, then
reproduce the failure.

??? note "Checking for nondeterminism"

    Run the same seed five times and diff the logs:

```bash
    for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do
      ingot sim dma -t burst_wrap -s 77120493 --json > run_$i.json
    done
    diff run_1.json run_2.json
```

    Any difference in the diagnostic output means the run isn't
    deterministic.
